{"prdMarkdown":"# PRD: Incremental Frontend Framework Migration\n\n## Problem\nThe Ledger API service currently renders UI exclusively via server-rendered Jinja templates (`templates/index.html`) served by Flask (`app.py`). This tightly couples frontend rendering to backend request-response cycles, limiting UI interactivity, requiring full-page reloads for user account and payment workflows, and constraining future client-side responsiveness.\n\n## Goals\n- Transition the application UI from server-rendered Jinja templates to a modern, responsive frontend framework.\n- Implement a gradual, page-by-page migration strategy that avoids disrupting current account and payment operations.\n- Provide backend JSON endpoints alongside existing server-rendered routes in `app.py` to serve dynamic UI components incrementally.\n\n## Non-goals\n- Full application or backend rewrite: Flask, Flask-SQLAlchemy, and Gunicorn as specified in `requirements.txt` will remain the backend stack.\n- Immediate total replacement of all templates in a single release.\n- Redesign of the underlying database schema or core business logic in `models.py`.\n\n## User Impact\n- End users will experience faster, reactive interactions without full-page reloads when interacting with migrated screens.\n- Navigation and form submissions (e.g., ledger views, payments) will provide immediate client-side feedback and smoother state updates.\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- Which specific user flow or page (e.g., the primary view in `templates/index.html`) should serve as the initial candidate for the gradual migration?\n- Are there specific client-side interaction or UI state requirements for user accounts and payments that dictate framework capabilities?","trdMarkdown":"# TRD: Incremental Frontend Framework Migration\n\n## Architecture Notes\n- **Hybrid Coexistence**: During migration, Flask routes in `app.py` will continue serving Jinja templates (`templates/index.html`) while progressively hosting mounted client-side framework components.\n- **API Layer**: New dedicated API endpoints (e.g., under `/api/...`) must be created in `app.py` returning JSON payloads to power client components.\n- **Serialization Strategy**: SQLAlchemy models in `models.py` currently do not possess serialization logic. Explicit dictionary serialization methods (`to_dict()`) or dedicated schemas must be implemented to safely expose account and payment data without leaking sensitive model attributes.\n- **Frontend Tooling & Delivery**: The repo currently lacks Node.js, `package.json`, or asset bundlers. The implementation must decide between a lightweight, buildless approach (e.g., Alpine.js or Vue via CDN/static script tags in `static/`) versus establishing a complete frontend build pipeline (e.g., Vite/Webpack).\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- **Toolchain Absence**: The repository contains only Python dependencies in `requirements.txt`. Introducing a framework requiring compilation requires introducing a separate Node.js build step into the deployment lifecycle.\n- **Template Delimiter Collisions**: Client-side frameworks utilizing `{{ }}` syntax (such as Vue or Angular) will collide with Jinja2 syntax in `templates/index.html` unless explicitly configured with alternative delimiters or wrapped in Jinja `{% raw %}` blocks.\n- **Serialization & Data Leaks in `models.py`**: Direct exposure of model instances to JSON without explicit field whitelisting introduces serialization errors and security risks.\n- **CSRF & Session Security**: Client-side asynchronous requests issued to `app.py` must send CSRF tokens and handle session-based authentication properly.\n- **Missing Dependencies**: `requirements.txt` currently only contains `flask==3.0.0`, `flask-sqlalchemy==3.1.1`, and `gunicorn==21.2.0`; serialization or CORS libraries may need to be introduced if a decoupled dev environment is used.\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- Should the frontend framework be integrated via a buildless approach (e.g., Alpine.js or Vue imported via static scripts) to keep the repository lightweight, or should an npm/Vite pipeline be introduced?\n- What serialization pattern or dependency (e.g., manual `to_dict()` methods in `models.py` vs. adding Marshmallow/Pydantic to `requirements.txt`) should be standardized for API responses?\n- How should CSRF tokens be injected into client-side `fetch` calls from within `templates/index.html`?\n\n## Estimate\n- **Estimated Effort**: 2–4 days for initial architecture setup, serialization pattern implementation, and pilot page migration; up to 8 days worst case if establishing an npm build pipeline and handling complex state synchronization."}